Line I'd like us to actually hold to, since it keeps coming up in review.

A cache write is an optimisation for the next run. Failing one makes the next=
 run more expensive, it does not make this run invalid. So nothing that has a=
lready cost the user money gets aborted because we couldn't put a file on dis=
k =E2=80=94 we take the hit on the rerun and carry on.

Wrote this up more fully in design/prompt-level-response-cache-keying.md. The=
 short version: if generation_params differ from the stored run, only those r=
ows go back out to the provider. The rest stay cached. Blowing the whole dir =
is the wrong default.

